0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 224 10 "Problem 3." }{TEXT 287 3 ".  " }}
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 287 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 287 56 "There i
s a one-to-one correspondence between subsets of " }{TEXT 225 17 "\{1,
 2, . . . , n\}" }{TEXT 287 28 " and binary lists of length " }{TEXT 
226 1 "n" }{TEXT 287 17 ", that is, lists " }{TEXT 227 3 "L =" }{TEXT 
287 1 " " }{TEXT 228 22 "[x1, x2 , . . . , xn] " }{TEXT 287 6 "where "
 }{TEXT 229 18 "x1, x2, . . . , xn" }{TEXT 287 25 " are elements of th
e set " }{TEXT 230 5 "\{0,1\}" }{TEXT 287 57 ".  The correspondence is
 given by associating to the set " }{TEXT 231 1 "S" }{TEXT 287 10 " th
e list " }{TEXT 232 1 "L" }{TEXT 287 7 " where " }{TEXT 233 34 "xi = 1
 if i is in S and 0 if not. " }{TEXT 287 78 "For example, the set \{1,
3,5\} corresponds to the list [1,0,1,0,1,0,0] if n = 7." }}{PARA 0 "" 
0 "" {TEXT 287 1 "\n" }{TEXT 234 3 "(a)" }{TEXT 287 19 " Write a proce
dure " }{TEXT 235 12 "list_to_set " }{TEXT 287 79 "whose input is a bi
nary list and whose output is the corresponding set. E. g., " }{TEXT 
236 24 "list_to_set([1,0,1,0,1])" }{TEXT 287 21 " will return the set 
" }{TEXT 237 7 "\{1,3,5\}" }{TEXT 287 44 ". Note that nops(L) is the l
ength of a list." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 287 1 "\n" }{TEXT 238 3 "(b)
" }{TEXT 287 19 " Write a procedure " }{TEXT 239 11 "set_to_list" }
{TEXT 287 22 " whose input is a pair" }{TEXT 240 4 " S,n" }{TEXT 287 
7 " where " }{TEXT 241 1 "S" }{TEXT 287 16 " is a subset of " }{TEXT 
242 17 "\{1, 2, . . . , n\}" }{TEXT 287 5 " and " }{TEXT 243 1 "n" }
{TEXT 287 69 " is a positive integer and whose output is the binary li
st of length " }{TEXT 244 1 "n" }{TEXT 287 26 " corresponding to the s
et " }{TEXT 245 1 "S" }{TEXT 287 23 ". E. g., if n = 5 then " }{TEXT 
246 22 "set_to_list(\{1,3,5\},5)" }{TEXT 287 13 " will return " }{TEXT
 247 11 "[1,0,1,0,1]" }{TEXT 287 2 ".\n" }{TEXT 287 1 "\n" }{TEXT 248 
3 "(c)" }{TEXT 287 59 " Show by a few tests that each procedure works.
 Then apply " }{TEXT 249 11 "set_to_list" }{TEXT 287 32 " to each set \+
in the powerset of " }{TEXT 250 12 "\{1, 2, 3, 4\}" }{TEXT 287 158 " t
o form all binary lists of length 4. Make a program to print out a tab
le of the following form. (But the order need not be the same as that \+
started below.)\n" }{TEXT 287 1 "\n" }{TEXT 287 24 "   [0,0,0,0] <--> \+
 \{  \}\n" }{TEXT 287 24 "   [1,0,0 0] <--> \{ 1 \}\n" }{TEXT 287 24 "
   [0,1,0,0] <--> \{ 2 \} " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 287 12 "    ......
.." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 287 7 "    etc" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
287 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 287 108 "Some extra commas in the out
put is okay. You may obtain the power set of the set \{1,2,...,n\} by \+
the command " }{TEXT 251 12 "powerset(n);" }{TEXT 287 37 " but you mus
t first load the package " }{TEXT 252 9 "combinat." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 
"> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 28 "list_to_set:=proc(n::list);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 
0 12 "a:=nops(n);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 7 "S:=\{\};\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 14 "fo
r i to a do\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 40 "if n[i]=1 then S:= S union \{i\}; end
 if;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "end do;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "return S;\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "end proc:" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 1 "\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 25 "list
_to_set([1,0,1,0,1]);" }}{PARA 207 "" 1 "" {TEXT 288 0 "" }{HYPERLNK 
288 "Warning, `a` is implicitly declared local to procedure `list_to_s
et`" 4 "http://www.maplesoft.com/support/help/errors/view.aspx?path=Wa
rning,%20%60a%60%20is%20implicitly%20declared%20local%20to%20procedure
%20%60list_to_set%60" "" }{TEXT 288 0 "" }}{PARA 207 "" 1 "" {TEXT 
288 0 "" }{HYPERLNK 288 "Warning, `S` is implicitly declared local to \+
procedure `list_to_set`" 4 "http://www.maplesoft.com/support/help/erro
rs/view.aspx?path=Warning,%20%60S%60%20is%20implicitly%20declared%20lo
cal%20to%20procedure%20%60list_to_set%60" "" }{TEXT 288 0 "" }}{PARA 
207 "" 1 "" {TEXT 288 0 "" }{HYPERLNK 288 "Warning, `i` is implicitly \+
declared local to procedure `list_to_set`" 4 "http://www.maplesoft.com
/support/help/errors/view.aspx?path=Warning,%20%60i%60%20is%20implicit
ly%20declared%20local%20to%20procedure%20%60list_to_set%60" "" }{TEXT 
288 0 "" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "<%\"\"\"\"\"$\"\"&" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "restart:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 29 
"set_to_list:=proc(s::set,n);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 13 "#a:=nops(s);\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 12 "#b:=max(s);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "#L:=[];\n" }{MPLTEXT 
1 0 22 "for i from 1 to n do \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 26 "  if i in s then L[
i]:=1;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 25 "  else L[i]:= 0; end if;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 
8 "end do;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 27 "return [seq(L[i],i=1..n)];\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "end proc:" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 0 "" }}{PARA 207 "" 1 "" 
{TEXT 288 0 "" }{HYPERLNK 288 "Warning, `i` is implicitly declared loc
al to procedure `set_to_list`" 4 "http://www.maplesoft.com/support/hel
p/errors/view.aspx?path=Warning,%20%60i%60%20is%20implicitly%20declare
d%20local%20to%20procedure%20%60set_to_list%60" "" }{TEXT 288 0 "" }}
{PARA 207 "" 1 "" {TEXT 288 0 "" }{HYPERLNK 288 "Warning, `L` is impli
citly declared local to procedure `set_to_list`" 4 "http://www.mapleso
ft.com/support/help/errors/view.aspx?path=Warning,%20%60L%60%20is%20im
plicitly%20declared%20local%20to%20procedure%20%60set_to_list%60" "" }
{TEXT 288 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 25 "set_to_lis
t(\{1,5,7\},10);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 4 "[r];" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 1 "\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 16 "r:=[1,2,3,4,5];\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 11 "has([r],2);" }}
{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "7,\"\"\"\"\"!F$F$F#F$F#F$F$F$" }}{PARA 
11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "7#I\"rG6\"" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 ">I
\"rG6\"7'\"\"\"\"\"#\"\"$\"\"%\"\"&" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "I
%trueG%*protectedG" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 253 11 "Problem  4
." }{TEXT 287 1 " " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 287 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 ""
 {TEXT 287 13 "Note that if " }{TEXT 254 1 "i" }{TEXT 287 20 " is an i
nteger then " }{TEXT 255 18 "convert(i,base, 2)" }{TEXT 287 14 " will \+
convert " }{TEXT 256 2 "i " }{TEXT 287 47 "to a binary list (the binar
y representation of " }{TEXT 257 1 "i" }{TEXT 287 37 " in reverse orde
r). Then you may use " }{TEXT 258 11 "list_to_set" }{TEXT 287 46 " to \+
convert  this list to a set. Note that if " }{TEXT 259 1 "i" }{TEXT 
287 11 " runs from " }{TEXT 260 1 "0" }{TEXT 287 4 " to " }{XPPEDIT 
205 0 "Typesetting:-mrow(Typesetting:-mi(\"\"), Typesetting:-mrow(Type
setting:-msup(Typesetting:-mn(\"2\", font_style_name = \"CharacterStyl
e256\", mathvariant = \"normal\"), Typesetting:-mi(\"n\", italic = \"t
rue\", font_style_name = \"CharacterStyle256\", mathvariant = \"italic
\"), superscriptshift = \"0\"), Typesetting:-mo(\"&minus;\", font_styl
e_name = \"CharacterStyle256\", mathvariant = \"normal\", fence = \"fa
lse\", separator = \"false\", stretchy = \"false\", symmetric = \"fals
e\", largeop = \"false\", movablelimits = \"false\", accent = \"false
\", lspace = \"0.2222222em\", rspace = \"0.2222222em\"), Typesetting:-
mn(\"1\", font_style_name = \"CharacterStyle256\", mathvariant = \"nor
mal\")), Typesetting:-mi(\"\"));" "-I%mrowG6#/I+modulenameG6\"I,Typese
ttingGI(_syslibGF'6%-I#miGF$6#Q!F'-F#6%-I%msupGF$6%-I#mnGF$6%Q\"2F'/%0
font_style_nameGQ2CharacterStyle256F'/%,mathvariantGQ'normalF'-F,6&Q\"
nF'/%'italicGQ%trueF'F8/F<Q'italicF'/%1superscriptshiftGQ\"0F'-I#moGF$
6.Q(&minus;F'F8F;/%&fenceGQ&falseF'/%*separatorGFO/%)stretchyGFO/%*sym
metricGFO/%(largeopGFO/%.movablelimitsGFO/%'accentGFO/%'lspaceGQ,0.222
2222emF'/%'rspaceGFhn-F56%Q\"1F'F8F;F+" }{TEXT 287 6 " then " }{TEXT 
261 32 "list_to_set(convert(i,base,2) ) " }{TEXT 287 33 " will run thr
ough all subsets of " }{TEXT 262 16 "\{1, 2, . . ., n\}" }{TEXT 287 3 
". \n" }{TEXT 287 1 "\n" }{TEXT 263 3 "(a)" }{TEXT 287 42 " Use this i
dea to make your own procedure " }{TEXT 264 9 "PowerSet " }{TEXT 287 
17 "which will given " }{TEXT 265 1 "n" }{TEXT 287 27 ",  produce the \+
powerset of " }{TEXT 266 17 "\{1, 2, . . . , n\}" }{TEXT 287 87 ". Sho
w by several examples that your procedure works. For your examples you
 should get " }{TEXT 267 24 "nops(PowerSet(n)) = 2^(n" }{TEXT 287 32 "
). Check that this is the case.\n" }{TEXT 287 2 " \n" }{TEXT 268 3 "(b
)" }{TEXT 287 35 " Use this idea to make a procedure " }{TEXT 269 7 "R
andSet" }{TEXT 287 43 " which given n produces a random subset of " }
{TEXT 270 17 "\{1, 2, . . . , n\}" }{TEXT 287 7 ". [Use " }{TEXT 271 
4 "rand" }{TEXT 287 42 " to produce a random integer in the range " }
{TEXT 272 8 "0..2^n-1" }{TEXT 287 36 " and then convert it to a subset
 of " }{TEXT 273 17 "\{1, 2, . . . , n\}" }{TEXT 287 1 "." }{TEXT 274 
3 "]  " }{TEXT 275 31 "Do NOT use PowerSet or powerset" }{TEXT 287 
240 ". Show by examples that it works for small n such as 5, 10, and 2
0 as well as for large n such as 100. It will even work for n = 1000, \+
but the output will be rather large. On average a random subset of \{1
,2,...,n\} will contain n/2 elements." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 276 7 "
[Remark" }{TEXT 287 62 ": The powerset of a set with 1000 elements con
tains more than " }{XPPEDIT 18 0 "Typesetting:-mrow(Typesetting:-mi(\"
\"), Typesetting:-mrow(Typesetting:-msup(Typesetting:-mn(\"10\", execu
table = \"false\", mathvariant = \"normal\"), Typesetting:-mn(\"300\",
 executable = \"false\", mathvariant = \"normal\"), superscriptshift =
 \"0\")), Typesetting:-mi(\"\"));" "-I%mrowG6#/I+modulenameG6\"I,Types
ettingGI(_syslibGF'6%-I#miGF$6#Q!F'-F#6#-I%msupGF$6%-I#mnGF$6%Q#10F'/%
+executableGQ&falseF'/%,mathvariantGQ'normalF'-F56%Q$300F'F8F;/%1super
scriptshiftGQ\"0F'F+" }{TEXT 287 171 " elements. This is, according to
 current estimates, more than the number of atoms in the whole univers
e. So you cannot expect your computer to be able to store such a set."
 }{TEXT 277 1 "]" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 0 "" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 205 10 "Problem 5." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 287 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 287 105 "Construct an original \+
